Warne,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on lodges, re=
serves and farms; we don't sell packages.

Buffalo Kloof carries black and white rhino, cheetah reintroduction and an an=
ti-poaching team with cameras and helicopter patrols. That operation depends =
on field communications: camera and sensor backhaul, ranger contact after dar=
k, and knowing a link has dropped before it matters. The connectivity that's =
described today stops at Spekboom's living area.

We've done a desktop terrain study of Buffalo Kloof from 30 m satellite eleva=
tion data and cloud-free imagery, over the mapped boundary of the Protected E=
nvironment (about 7,500 ha). It's attached. What it found:

- Two ridge high sites inside the boundary, one at about 360 m in the north a=
nd one at about 290 m in the south, 7.7 km apart with a clear path between th=
em and clearance to spare.
- A candidate carrier uplink of 12.1 km from the Makhanda high ground into th=
e northern site, also clear. Six mapped communication masts sit within 25 km,=
 but none is tagged with an operator in public data, so the actual Vodacom si=
te is the first thing we confirm with Vodacom before any design.
- About 48% of the 75 km2 reserve is in line of sight of those two sites at f=
ield-terminal height. The Kowie valley floor is where the rest sits, so a thi=
rd site or a relay may be needed once we know where Spekboom, Acacia and Khat=
u actually are.

None of the camps has a published position, so the study lists them as positi=
ons to confirm; a marked map or KMZ from you would let us add them the same d=
ay. The site positions are desktop candidates until we walk them.

What this means for the reserve, in plain terms: today the field is dark and =
each camp rents its own signal. With an owned backbone the cameras, the fence=
 and the rangers are on one monitored network, day and night, and every new c=
amera or sensor is an endpoint on infrastructure the reserve already owns rat=
her than a new provider contract.

The next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you have, what fails, wh=
at it costs you, and what an owned network would look like, with the high sit=
es and the uplink confirmed on the ground. R3,500 ex VAT, credited against th=
e build, and the report is yours either way. If you share the camps' current =
connectivity invoices, I'll put Buffalo Kloof's actual payback in that report.

Can I call you for 10 minutes this week?

Gerhard Smit
Director | CTTX Services (Pty) Ltd
gerhard@cttx.co.za | 041 371 1089 | 084 550 3281
www.cttx.co.za

If this isn't relevant to you, reply "no thanks" and I won't follow up.

